

     This is an improved version of the directory listing program
distributed with OS/8 Versions 3/3C.  It is functionally equivalent
to the original version and includes any updates which have been
published up to the time of submission.  To distiguish it from the
earlier versions it has been designated 'V5'.  The loading and op-
erating instructions in the OS/8 Handbook apply without change to
this version.*

     The improvements are as follows:

1.   Multiple column listings are now printed in column order rather
than row order.  This makes the output vastly easier to read, since,
for instance, adjacent empty areas now appear juxtaposed in the out-
put just as they do in a PIP "/E" listing.

2.  The listing can be alphabetized!  This is especially convenient
for dumping the directory of a large disk where the actual program
arrangement is not very significant and the number of programs is
usually so large that it is difficult to quickly locate a given file.

3.  The dates may be printed in either a mixed alphanumeric form (as
in the original version) or in a straight numeric style compatible
with the monitor DATE command.  There is a provision for extending
the date beyond 1977.

4.  The header block defined by the DECSYSTEM-8 program PARAM.SV can
be listed at the beginning to provide a convenient summary sheet
showing both the volume identification and the program contents, all
in one place.

5.  There are fewer spaces between columns so that the output will
fit on standard output devices (location 13163 controls the column
spacing).  An extra space is inserted after the block number so that
it does not run into the file size, but when selecting the numeric
date option each column has 1 less character.  The formfeed at the
end-of-output may be eliminated to reduce waste paper or to preserve
the display on some types of CRT terminals.

     These features are selected by the /A, /H, /N and /P options.
"/A" specifies Alphabetizing.  If /A is used, /B and /E options will
be ignored.  "/N" selects Numeric dates while "/P" Prevents Paging.
"/H" prints out the Header information.  If the device does not have
a parameter block, /H will be ignored.

     Acknowledgments:  The initial improvements were made by Larry
Fowler of the Boeing Commercial Airplane Co. in Seattle, Washington.
He added the /A and /H options and also modified the source file so
that different I/O codes could be substituted for the terminal.  Dr.
Thomas W. McIntyre of the West Virginia University Medical Center in
Morgantown, W. V. added the column-output feature and Jim van Zee of
the Chemistry Department at the University of Washington in Seattle
added the /N and /P options and condensed the code so that it would
fit into the original file space.

*Loading Instructions:   .DA 12/15/76   (Delete the original version
(Change location 14713   .R ABSLDR       first so this one will fit)
to 2426 to make TV: the  *PTR:(89P)=14600$
default output device)   .SA SYS DIRECT

